<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Rasulid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1229 CE – 1454 CE · capital Ta'izz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most brilliant medieval dynasty of Yemen, whose scholar-sultans ruled from Ta'izz, grew rich on Aden's trade and built famous madrasas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din-umar-ibn-rasul">Nur al-Din Umar ibn Rasul</a>, a commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> origin, was left as deputy when the last ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ayyubid-yemen">Ayyubid Yemen</a> departed in 1229. He soon declared himself sultan and obtained recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 1235, founding a dynasty based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muzaffar-yusuf-i">al-Muzaffar Yusuf</a>, who reigned for 45 years and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> in 1279, the state reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca's</a> borders to Dhofar. The Rasulids taxed the spice and textile trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> fleets in the 15th century. Several sultans were scholars: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-umar-ii">al-Ashraf Umar</a> wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, medicine and farming. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> gained mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the late 14th century revolts by slave troops, tribes and family rivals weakened the dynasty. After disputed successions in the 1440s, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-sultanate">Tahirid Sultanate</a> took power in 1454. The Rasulid period is remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen's</a> medieval golden age.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rasulid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Rasulid dynasty" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Tiwahi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rasulid_1264.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1229 CE – 1454 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ta'izz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nur al-Din Umar, al-Muzaffar Yusuf, al-Ashraf Umar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen, Saudi Arabia, Oman</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rasulids, Rasulid Sultanate</dd></dl></aside></div>
